course: dictionary
lesson: 7742
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and pump is one of them. Read the words around it to find out which meaning the writer means. A pump is a machine that moves water, air, fuel or gas from one place to another.
explain: Pump can also be a verb, which means to move something with a pump. «Pump up a tyre» means fill the tyre with air. Check the sentence: is someone doing the action, or is a machine being named?
explain: Your heart is a pump too. «It squeezes about 70 to 100 times a minute and pumps blood» around your body. Pumped can also mean excited, like feeling pumped before a big match.
fact: Pump can be a noun, which is a thing, or a verb, which is an action.
fact: A pump is a machine that pushes water, air, fuel or gas from one place to another.
fact: «Pump up a tyre» means fill the tyre with air using a pump.
fact: Your heart is a pump: it pushes blood through tubes called blood vessels to every part of your body.
fact: Early steam engines were built to do real jobs, such as «pumping water out of mines».
exercise: In «Dad snaps "Phone off!" at the petrol pump», what is a petrol pump? | A bicycle tool | A kind of tyre | A small flat shoe | *A machine that pushes petrol into a car
exercise: «It squeezes about 70 to 100 times a minute and pumps blood.» What is doing the pumping? | *Your heart | Your ears | Your legs | A bicycle
exercise: Jamal says he is pumped for the match. What does pumped mean? | *Excited and full of energy | Tired and sleepy | Wet through | Lost and confused
test: «Early steam engines were built to do real jobs, such as pumping water out of mines.» What is pumping in this sentence? | Cooking food | Painting the mines | *Moving water out of mines with a machine | Burning coal in a fire
test: «The pump is broken.» Is pump a thing or an action here? | Neither | *A thing, a machine | An action | Both at once
exam: «The pump in the garage is old, so Dad will pump up the tyres.» How is pump used in each part? | First as an action, then as a machine | Both times as an action | Both times as a machine | *First as a machine, then as an action
